  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nFriday night and Saturday, a trough off the west coast will dig \nsouth, absorbing the remnants of Hurricane Rachel and pushing a \nridge through West Texas and Southeast New Mexico Saturday.  This \nwill result in a gradual warming trend through the weekend as \nleeside troughing on the Front Range increases return flow locally. \nTemperatures for this forecast will peak Sunday, when highs top out \n~ 10-15 F above normal, putting most areas in the 90s.  Meanwhile, a \nsecondary, stronger trough will be digging down the west coast, \nincreasing progressive southwest flow aloft over the area on the \nbackside of the exiting ridge.  The trough is forecast to make \nlandfall in SoCal Monday night, and move east into the Rockies by \nWednesday afternoon.  The next effect of this will be decreasing \nthicknesses after Sunday, with highs rounding out the extended \nWednesday afternoon at a mere 5-10 F above normal, putting most \nparts back in the 80s.  Sometime during the first part of next week, \nthe secondary trough is forecast to absorb the remnants of T.S. \nSimon, and move a slug of tropical moisture through West Texas and \nSoutheast New Mexico. Current model runs suggest this will occur \nTuesday evening or so.  GFS forecast soundings increase PWATs at \nKMAF to 1.58\" by 06Z Wednesday, while ECMWF soundings increase to \n1.78\" by 00Z Wednesday.  Climatology puts the 100th percentile at \n12Z Wednesday at 1.12\", so a decent rainfall event could be shaping \nup, and bears watching.  QPF could be better, but this will increase \nif it pans out.  However, it's always a good bet that this will dry \nout in a model run or two.  Finally, should the LREF pan out w/the \ntrack/timing of the trough, high winds will be possible in the \nGuadalupes/Delawares Wednesday afternoon.",
